The wind was a wild thing that night, tearing at the lighthouse glass and howling like a lost soul. Elias, keeper for forty years, knew its voice better than his own. He was just finishing his rounds, the beam of the great lamp sweeping its steady, reassuring arc across the churning black, when he saw it—a glint of something pale tumbling in the foam at the base of the rocks.

Curiosity was a luxury he rarely afforded himself, but the sea had a way of offering gifts when you least expected them. He pulled on his oilskin, the fabric stiff with salt, and descended the spiral stairs. The air outside was a wet slap to the face. He scrambled down the slick stone path, his boots finding familiar footholds, until he was close enough to see it: a bottle, dark green and crusted with barnacles, caught in a crevice.

He snatched it before a wave could reclaim it, and back in the warm, humming heart of the lighthouse, he held it up to the lamp. Inside, a single sheet of paper, curled and yellowed. The cork was stubborn, swollen with seawater. He wrestled with it, his gnarled fingers working the glass until it finally popped free with a satisfying *thock*.

He unrolled the paper on his small wooden table, smoothing out the damp edges. The ink was faded, a spidery scrawl that had fought a long war with the sea.

*
